From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 04 Nov 2026 09:42:09 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: sc8547_svooc: keep the CPUs out of deep idle
 during a session

A SuperVOOC adapter asks the phone a question every 45 ms and waits a
few milliseconds for the answer, which this driver gives from the
PHY's interrupt thread over I2C (two bulk transfers, then the pump's
status and ADC block). The factory AP VOOC PHY driver holds a CPU
latency QoS request of 0 for the whole fast-charge session, taken when
the session starts and again before every PHY register access, and
released when the session ends (oplus_voocphy_pm_qos_update() in
oplus_ap_voocphy.c, which on kernels since 5.10 turns any value into
cpu_latency_qos_add_request(&req, 0)); it also floors the CPU
frequencies (1.79/2.44 GHz) for 15 s from the start of a session and
for the length of each current change (100 ms on this board). This
driver held neither.

On the OnePlus Pad 4 the omission reset the tablet, silently, twice in
a row: 6.2 A sessions on the 55 W factory adapter (model 0x24) died
four to five minutes in with every reading steady to the last second,
the charger firmware log normal and nothing issued by the kernel. A
frame-level I2C trace of the next sessions showed what happens at that
moment: the adapter, held at its 5.1 A ceiling by a request above its
class, lowers its output one level exactly 300 s after it granted fast
charge (9.03 to 8.67 V on the bus in one frame, the battery current
9.4 to 7.3 A), with nothing on the wire before it. Two sessions that
crossed that step at the same power with every cpuidle state above WFI
disabled by hand went on to finish. The kernel this port runs carries
the hard lockup and CSD lock wait detectors that its maintainer enabled
after the same freezes "while charging over SuperVOOC", whose trace
showed the I2C transfers to both pumps timing out and then the charger
firmware, RPMh and two CPUs stopping within 8 s: the pumps' interrupts
and transfers arriving into a cluster that had powered down.

Take a CPU latency QoS request of 0 when a session enters IDENTIFY,
after the wakeup source, and release it in svooc_restore(), after the
buck input is back, and in the cleanup path, so no CPU enters a state
deeper than WFI from the adapter's first frame to the end of the
session. This is the factory driver's hold; its frequency floors are
left out, as the reply took at most 1.8 ms from interrupt to TX write
without them, in the sessions that finished and in the two that reset.

The two sessions that survived the step had the states disabled through
sysfs, which is the same constraint this request places; the module
with the request is flown next, on the same adapter and power.

Signed-off-by: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/sc8547_svooc.c |   17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/sc8547_svooc.c b/drivers/power/supply/sc8547_svooc.c
index 841ea468f5fc..4a26951f996d 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/sc8547_svooc.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/sc8547_svooc.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/mux/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/pm.h>
+#include <linux/pm_qos.h>
 #include <linux/pm_wakeup.h>
 #include <linux/power/oplus_svooc.h>
 #include <linux/power/sc8547.h>
@@ -147,6 +148,8 @@ struct sc8547_svooc {
 	struct notifier_block notifier;
 	struct delayed_work work;
 	struct dentry *debugfs;
+	/* Deep CPU idle states held off while the adapter talks. */
+	struct pm_qos_request latency;
 	/* Serializes the session between the PHY interrupt and the worker. */
 	struct mutex lock;
 	/* The adapter protocol of the session, chosen when it starts. */
@@ -710,6 +713,16 @@ static void svooc_start(struct sc8547_svooc *sv)
 	}
 
 	pm_stay_awake(sv->dev);
+	/*
+	 * The adapter asks every 45 ms and gives the reply milliseconds, from
+	 * the PHY's interrupt thread over I2C. The factory driver keeps every
+	 * CPU out of its deep idle states for the whole session; without that,
+	 * this tablet reset silently twice, four to five minutes into a
+	 * session, when the adapter stepped its output and the pumps raised
+	 * interrupts into a cluster that had powered down.
+	 */
+	if (!cpu_latency_qos_request_active(&sv->latency))
+		cpu_latency_qos_add_request(&sv->latency, 0);
 	mutex_lock(&sv->lock);
 	sv->state = SVOOC_IDENTIFY;
 	sv->error = 0;
@@ -985,6 +998,8 @@ static void svooc_restore(struct sc8547_svooc *sv)
 	mutex_lock(&sv->lock);
 	sv->state = SVOOC_DONE;
 	mutex_unlock(&sv->lock);
+	if (cpu_latency_qos_request_active(&sv->latency))
+		cpu_latency_qos_remove_request(&sv->latency);
 	pm_relax(sv->dev);
 	power_supply_changed(sv->psy);
 	/* The adapter may already be gone. */
@@ -1206,6 +1221,8 @@ static void svooc_cleanup(void *data)
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&sv->work);
 	if (sv->state == SVOOC_STOPPING)
 		svooc_restore(sv);
+	if (cpu_latency_qos_request_active(&sv->latency))
+		cpu_latency_qos_remove_request(&sv->latency);
 	sc8547_set_phy_handler(sv->main, NULL, NULL);
 	debugfs_remove_recursive(sv->debugfs);
 	device_init_wakeup(sv->dev, false);
